 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Encoding is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel is processed once in a block [G]. Vector quantization is $O(N \cdot K)$ where $K=256$ is the codebook size, which can be optimized with search algorithms [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The compressed representation is $1/4$ the size of the original volume in terms of texels (since each $4^3$ block is represented by one texel in $I$), plus the size of the codebooks ($256 \times 64 + 256 \times 8$ floats/bytes) and the address texture ($4^3$) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Designed for hardware acceleration using dependent texture fetches on GPUs (e.g., ATI 9700) [S1, unofficial]. Decoding is efficient as it involves simple texture lookups and additions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High compression ratio for homogeneous data due to thresholding; hardware-friendly decoding; multi-resolution access via the hierarchical structure [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Fixed block size limits adaptability; vector quantization requires pre-computed codebooks; lossy compression introduces artifacts in non-homogeneous regions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time volume rendering on hardware with limited bandwidth and texture units; scenes with large homogeneous regions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Scenes with fine, high-frequency details throughout; lossless compression requirements [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Encoding involves block-wise averaging and vector quantization. Vector quantization requires computing distances to all 256 codewords for each block, which is $O(N \cdot 256)$ where $N$ is the number of blocks. Decoding is $O(1)$ per voxel using texture lookups [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The compressed representation requires $1/4$ the number of voxels for the index texture (3 bytes per voxel) plus the codebook textures ($256 \times 64 \times 4$ bytes + $256 \times 8 \times 4$ bytes + $4^3 \times 2$ bytes for address texture) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** The method was tested on an ATI 9700 GPU, exploiting per-fragment arithmetic and dependent texture fetches [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Hardware-friendly decoding via texture lookups; enhanced fidelity by assembling signals from separate frequency bands; efficient representation of homogeneous regions via thresholding [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Lossy compression; requires pre-computed codebooks; block artifacts may occur at boundaries; limited to $4^3$ block sizes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When rendering volumetric data on hardware that supports dependent texture fetches and vector quantization codebooks, and when memory bandwidth is a bottleneck [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When lossless compression is required; when the data does not benefit from spatial block decomposition [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Triadic frequency band decomposition was introduced in the context of compression domain volume rendering, specifically targeting hardware acceleration on early GPU architectures like the ATI 9700 [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to leverage the capabilities of dependent texture fetches and per-fragment arithmetic operations for efficient on-chip decoding [S1, unofficial]. The method is part of a broader family of wavelet and transform-based compression techniques for volumetric data [S1, unofficial].
+Triadic frequency band decomposition, also known as hierarchical quantization scheme, was introduced in the context of compression domain volume rendering. It was presented in a paper titled "Compression Domain Volume Rendering" by researchers associated with the Technical University of Munich (TUM) [S1, unofficial]. The method exploits OpenGL API features on ATI hardware to perform efficient on-chip decoding [S1, unofficial].
 
